Step 2: Convert Docs → Chunks
Why chunking?


LLM embeddings work best on small text blocks


Enables precise retrieval


Each document was:


Read


Split into logical chunks


Stored individually



 Step 3: Create Embeddings (Vectorization)
Each chunk was converted into numerical vectors using Vertex AI embeddings.
Conceptually:
"Cloud Run containers must listen on PORT 8080"
→ [0.021, -0.057, 0.039, ...]

These vectors capture semantic meaning, not keywords.

 Step 4: Store Chunks + Vectors in Firestore
You created:
Firestore
└── devops_rag_chunks (collection)
    ├── doc_id
    │   ├── text
    │   ├── embedding
    │   ├── source

 Screenshot confirms data exists
 This is your vector database

 Step 5: RAG Search Logic (Critical)
When user asks:

"Why is my Cloud Run service failing to start?"

Your RAG tool does:


Convert question → embedding


Compare question embedding with stored vectors


Find most similar chunks


Return top-K results


 This is semantic search, not keyword search.

 Step 6: RAG Tool (devops_rag_search)
This tool:


Reads Firestore


Computes similarity


Returns matching chunks with source paths


You tested it directly:
devops_rag_search("Why is my Cloud Run service failing to start?")

 Output showed:


Cloud Run troubleshooting


Kubernetes crashloop


CI/CD failures



 Step 7: Connect RAG Tool to ADK Agent
This is the big ADK advantage.
Instead of hardcoding:


You registered RAG as a tool


ADK decides:


When to call RAG


How to inject results into prompt


How to compose final answer


 This is agentic behavior, not simple prompt calls.

